WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
How do animals see the world?

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.090
Animals see the world differently than we do. Completely differently.

3
00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:12.810
Colors, shapes and even the sense of time — everything differs from the human one.

4
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:15.490
And scientists, by analyzing eyes and the nervous system,

5
00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.130
have established roughly how animals see the world.

6
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.490
Animals not only see the world differently,

7
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.450
but time also flows differently for them.

8
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.290
Every living creature has its own perception of time,

9
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.210
that is, the critical flicker fusion frequency.

10
00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:29.890
In English — CFF.

11
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.670
It shows how fast light must flicker

12
00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.650
for a living creature to stop noticing the flicker.

13
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.830
And what determines what our CFF will be is the speed of our metabolism.

14
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.490
For humans this number is 60.

15
00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.850
That's exactly why our electric current has a frequency of 60 Hz —

16
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.230
so that the flicker of a light bulb is imperceptible.

17
00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.310
And with a CFF of 60 Hz we see the world at our speed.

18
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:54.030
At the same time, for animals with a higher CFF everything is slowed down,

19
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.050
and for animals with a lower CFF everything is sped up.

20
00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.270
That's exactly why your dog can't watch a video on a 60 Hz phone.

21
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.590
Because dogs see with a CFF above 80 Hz.

22
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.810
And no, nobody asked the dog to tell us this —

23
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.950
we have a device called a retinograph.

24
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:14.790
It measures how many light flickers are needed for the eye to stop noticing the change.

25
00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.390
That's how we know this for every animal that has vision.

26
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.870
For the rest — not so much.

27
00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.810
And now the most interesting part.

28
00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.210
Let's start right away with dogs.

29
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.570
Vision in dogs differs because of the eye itself.

30
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.310
While humans have three types of photoreceptors, dogs have only two.

31
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.730
So dogs see a smaller spectrum of colors,

32
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.690
namely only blue and yellow.

33
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.050
Besides, their eye focuses poorly.

34
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.230
So they don't see a quite sharp picture, only blurred movements.

35
00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.250
And everything else they recognize thanks to an exceptional sense of smell.

36
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.830
Their perception of time is 80 Hz.

37
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.450
30% more than ours.

38
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.770
So for them the world looks like this.

39
00:01:47.770 --> 00:02:05.030
And what about cats?

40
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.350
Cats are hunters.

41
00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:07.590
They're very fast.

42
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.850
And most of you would say their CFF is even higher than dogs',

43
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.630
and that they see in even more slow motion.

44
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.190
Wrong.

45
00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:17.110
Believe it or not, cats' CFF is lower even than ours.

46
00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:18.750
And it's 50 Hz.

47
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.470
So cats see us slightly sped up.

48
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.810
As for colors, they see us the same way dogs do.

49
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.230
Only their eye structure is better adapted for focusing,

50
00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.030
so they see much sharper than dogs.

51
00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.510
And unlike both humans and dogs,

52
00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.070
cats have more photoreceptors for light.

53
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.270
So they have excellent night vision.

54
00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:37.970
And this is how they see the world.

55
00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.270
We gave priority to cats and dogs because that's the most interesting to most people.

56
00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.710
And now we move on to birds.

57
00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.610
Almost all birds have as many as 4 types of photoreceptors.

58
00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.270
So they see many more colors than humans.

59
00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.170
Even ultraviolet.

60
00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.430
Their focus is much better than a human's,

61
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.030
and even at great distances they see the tiniest details.

62
00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.950
They also have good peripheral vision,

63
00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:16.010
which makes them undisputed winners when it comes to vision.

64
00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.090
Their CFF differs slightly.

65
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.730
It varies by species, but mostly it's around 120 Hz.

66
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:25.330
With the exception of, for example, the hummingbird, which reaches 200 Hz.

67
00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.730
So most birds see like this.

68
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.900
Those of you who have a hamster are probably wondering how rodents see.

69
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.920
Like most mammals, they have two types of photoreceptors,

70
00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.700
and they see mostly blue and yellow.

71
00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.180
Only they have the most developed light receptors.

72
00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.600
So in the darkest rooms they see exceptionally well.

73
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.220
Although they see great in the dark, vision isn't their strongest side,

74
00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.300
and they rely on other senses for more than 90%.

75
00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.580
And as for CFF, there's a big split here.

76
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.900
While for hamsters and squirrels it's around 80 Hz,

77
00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.340
rats perceive time at just 40 Hz.

78
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.620
That means we're sped up for them too

79
00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.660
and that everything happens very fast for them.

80
00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.120
This is actually strange, because they're very fast and hard to catch.

81
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.580
As for squirrels and hamsters, it looks roughly like this.

82
00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.600
Reptiles interest me most.

83
00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.680
They don't have a constant CFF:

84
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.380
as the temperature changes, the speed of their metabolism changes too.

85
00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:02.920
And so does their CFF.

86
00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.040
When they're warm, they see things sped up,

87
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.240
and when it's cold, everything is slowed down for them.

88
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.280
There are too many species to describe, so I can't make an example.

89
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.100
Some reptiles see colors, and some only black and white.

90
00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.800
In general, their vision isn't that developed,

91
00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.860
and they perceive time depending on temperature.

92
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.720
With insects almost everything is different.

93
00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.540
They can have several eyes.

94
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.300
Many more eyes.

95
00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:30.040
These are their lenses, and each little part gathers information on its own.

96
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.940
After that their brain joins all these parts into one big picture,

97
00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.360
but of rather poor quality.

98
00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.860
They do see colors, though in a spectrum that differs greatly from ours.

99
00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:42.160
They see blue, green and ultraviolet,

100
00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.400
which helps bees find a flower much more easily.

101
00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.320
For insects like the gnat, CFF reaches as high as 270 Hz,

102
00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.040
which means they live life in very, very slow motion.

103
00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.560
All this looks to them roughly like this.

104
00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.180
I'll name a few animals with an exceptionally low CFF,

105
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.820
which perceive everything very fast and can live very, very long.

106
00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:19.980
In fish living at great depth, CFF can be less than 1,

107
00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.780
which means they see very slowed down and live very long.

108
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.300
Elephants have a CFF of about 30 Hz,

109
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.360
meaning they also see us as sped-up video, but not as much as fish.

110
00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.180
An animal known for living very long,

111
00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.740
and whose CFF is only 10 Hz, is the land tortoise.

112
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.640
It knows how not to stress.

113
00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.280
So that I know whether you find this interesting,

114
00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.780
you can support the video by clicking any button below,

115
00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.320
and write if you have an interesting question or idea.

116
00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:46.800
Thanks for following the channel.